{"parentUuid":"1c9c5630-cac8-4c67-99e1-5bcdec7e8a38","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"mcp_instructions_delta","addedNames":["wporg-mcp-server"],"addedBlocks":["## wporg-mcp-server\nWordPress.org MCP Server — provides tools, resources, and prompts for interacting with WordPress.org services.\n\nUse prompts/list to discover available workflows for specific tasks. Browse wporg://* resources for reference documentation."],"removedNames":[]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"4e723f00-2f74-47c1-a0a7-2dbb32313341","timestamp":"2026-06-04T08:22:28.924Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gaelrobin/Local Sites/bwu/app/public/wp-content/plugins/backwpup-pro","sessionId":"552cd3ab-4013-45e4-af41-463cd6c8a302","version":"2.1.162","gitBranch":"chore/improve-assistance"}
{"parentUuid":"4e723f00-2f74-47c1-a0a7-2dbb32313341","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"skill_listing","content":"- backwpup-architecture: Use this skill when changing service structure, shared logic, bootstrapping, dependency wiring, or any code that may affect FREE/PRO boundaries in BackWPup.\n- backwpup-frontend-architecture: Use this skill when changing admin JavaScript, CSS, or HTML templates in BackWPUp Pro.\n- sprint-shortlister: Generate a sprint shortlist for wp-media/backwpup-pro with fast story point estimation and capacity planning.\n\nUse this skill whenever you need to:\n- Plan a 2-week sprint for BackWPUp Pro with realistic team capacity constraints\n- Quickly estimate effort on unstarted issues without full grooming analysis\n- Create a balanced shortlist across bugs, features, and tech debt\n- Generate a shareable HTML report for sprint planning meetings\n\nThe skill asks for team size (default: 2 developers), optionally a target milestone, then fetches open unassigned issues from the repo. It estimates story points through lightweight scope analysis, builds a prioritized shortlist to fit the sprint capacity, and outputs an interactive HTML report ready to share with stakeholders. The entire process takes 2–3 minutes.\n- changelog: Generate a changelog entry from merged PRs since the last git tag. Use this when preparing a release, drafting release notes, or summarizing what changed between versions — even if the user says \"what changed since last release\" or \"help me write release notes\". Invoke with `/changelog` (since last tag) or `/changelog v3.x.x` (since a specific tag).\n- checkout: List all your unread GitHub notifications, grouped by priority and type (mention, review requested, assigned, etc.) with clickable URLs. Use this as a quick daily triage of everything waiting for your attention on GitHub — like checking github.com/notifications but from the terminal. Invoke with `/checkout`.\n- estimate: Estimate the complexity of a GitHub issue for sprint planning. Use this whenever someone wants to size a ticket, groom a backlog item, plan a sprint, or get a sense of effort — even if they just say \"how big is this?\" or \"is this a quick fix?\". Invoke with `/estimate <issue-number>` or `/estimate <GitHub issue URL>`.\n- skill-audit: Audit all Claude skill files for stale references, ambiguous triggers, and hardcoded values. Use this periodically to keep the skill library healthy — invoke with `/skill-audit`. Reports PASS / WARN / FAIL per skill. Does NOT auto-fix anything.\n- standup: Generate a standup update from yesterday's git activity and PR status. Use this whenever you need to prepare a standup message, daily update, or summary of recent work — even if the user just says \"what did I do yesterday\" or \"help me write my standup\". Invoke with `/standup` (for yesterday) or `/standup YYYY-MM-DD` (for a specific date).\n- standup-global: Generate a standup update from your GitHub activity across ALL repositories — not just the current project. Use this instead of /standup when you work across multiple repos and want a full picture of yesterday's work. Invoke with `/standup-global` (for yesterday) or `/standup-global YYYY-MM-DD` (for a specific date).\n- deep-research: Deep research harness — fan-out web searches, fetch sources, adversarially verify claims, synthesize a cited report. - When the user wants a deep, multi-source, fact-checked research report on any topic. BEFORE invoking, check if the question is specific enough to research directly — if underspecified (e.g., \"what car to buy\" without budget/use-case/region), ask 2-3 clarifying questions to narrow scope. Then pass the refined question as args, weaving the answers in.\n- maestro:docs: Update developer-facing documentation to reflect code changes on the current branch. Runs as an inline skill inside backend-agent and frontend-agent (step 2.5 of the internal sequence) after implementation and before DOD. Receives the explicit list of changed files from the implementation agent. No-op if no public API changes occurred.\n- maestro:dod\n- maestro:e2e\n- maestro:issue-workflow\n- maestro:knowledge-graph\n- maestro:orchestrator\n- maestro:wordpress-compliance\n- agents-observe:observe: Agents Observe dashboard and server management\n- update-config: Use this skill to configure the Claude Code harness via settings.json. Automated behaviors (\"from now on when X\", \"each time X\", \"whenever X\", \"before/after X\") require hooks configured in settings.json - the harness executes these, not Claude, so memory/preferences cannot fulfill them. Also use for: permissions (\"allow X\", \"add permission\", \"move permission to\"), env vars (\"set X=Y\"), hook troubleshooting, or any changes to settings.json/settings.local.json files. Examples: \"allow npm commands\", \"add bq permission to global settings\", \"move permission to user settings\", \"set DEBUG=true\", \"when claude stops show X\". For simple settings like theme/model, suggest the /config command.\n- keybindings-help: Use when the user wants to customize keyboard shortcuts, rebind keys, add chord bindings, or modify ~/.claude/keybindings.json. Examples: \"rebind ctrl+s\", \"add a chord shortcut\", \"change the submit key\", \"customize keybindings\".\n- verify: Verify that a code change actually does what it's supposed to by running the app and observing behavior. Use when asked to verify a PR, confirm a fix works, test a change manually, check that a feature works, or validate local changes before pushing.\n- code-review: Review the current diff for correctness bugs and reuse/simplification/efficiency cleanups at the given effort level (low/medium: fewer, high-confidence findings; high→max: broader coverage, may include uncertain findings). Pass --comment to post findings as inline PR comments, or --fix to apply the findings to the working tree after the review.\n- simplify: Review the changed code for reuse, simplification, efficiency, and altitude cleanups, then apply the fixes. Quality only — it does not hunt for bugs; use /code-review for that.\n- fewer-permission-prompts: Scan your transcripts for common read-only Bash and MCP tool calls, then add a prioritized allowlist to project .claude/settings.json to reduce permission prompts.\n- loop: Run a prompt or slash command on a recurring interval (e.g. /loop 5m /foo). Omit the interval to let the model self-pace. - When the user wants to set up a recurring task, poll for status, or run something repeatedly on an interval (e.g. \"check the deploy every 5 minutes\", \"keep running /babysit-prs\"). Do NOT invoke for one-off tasks.\n- schedule: Create, update, list, or run scheduled remote agents (routines) that execute on a cron schedule. - When the user wants to schedule a recurring remote agent, set up automated tasks, create a cron job for Claude Code, or manage their scheduled agents/routines. Also use when the user wants a one-time scheduled run (\"run this once at 3pm\", \"remind me to check X tomorrow\").\n- claude-api: Build, debug, and optimize Claude API / Anthropic SDK apps. Apps built with this skill should include prompt caching. Also handles migrating existing Claude API code between Claude model versions (4.5 → 4.6, 4.6 → 4.7, retired-model replacements).\nTRIGGER when: code imports `anthropic`/`@anthropic-ai/sdk`; user asks for the Claude API, Anthropic SDK, or Managed Agents; user adds/modifies/tunes a Claude feature (caching, thinking, compaction, tool use, batch, files, citations, memory) or model (Opus/Sonnet/Haiku) in a file; questions about prompt caching / cache hit rate in an Anthropic SDK project.\nSKIP: file imports `openai`/other-provider SDK, filename like `*-openai.py`/`*-generic.py`, provider-neutral code, general programming/ML.\n- run: Launch and drive this project's app to see a change working. Use when asked to run, start, or screenshot the app, or to confirm a change works in the real app (not just tests). First looks for a project skill that already covers launching the app; otherwise falls back to built-in patterns per project type (CLI, server, TUI, Electron, browser-driven, library).\n- init\n- review\n- security-review","skillCount":31,"isInitial":true,"names":["backwpup-architecture","backwpup-frontend-architecture","sprint-shortlister","changelog","checkout","estimate","skill-audit","standup","standup-global","deep-research","maestro:docs","maestro:dod","maestro:e2e","maestro:issue-workflow","maestro:knowledge-graph","maestro:orchestrator","maestro:wordpress-compliance","agents-observe:observe","update-config","keybindings-help","verify","code-review","simplify","fewer-permission-prompts","loop","schedule","claude-api","run","init","review","security-review"]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"377300e6-1d8f-46ee-89db-03a06c2fa220","timestamp":"2026-06-04T08:22:28.924Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gaelrobin/Local Sites/bwu/app/public/wp-content/plugins/backwpup-pro","sessionId":"552cd3ab-4013-45e4-af41-463cd6c8a302","version":"2.1.162","gitBranch":"chore/improve-assistance"}
